## HA configuration for Alertmanager

There is no option on this chart to set up Alertmanager with [HA mode](https://github.com/prometheus/alertmanager#high-availability).
To enable the HA configuration, you can use:
- [VictoriaMetrics Operator](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/operator/)
- official [Alertmanager Helm chart](https://github.com/prometheus-community/helm-charts/tree/main/charts/alertmanager)

{{ include "chart.uninstallSection" . }}

### HTTP listen address

The chart configures the VMAlert HTTP listen address via `.Values.server.http` list.
Each entry in the list configures a separate HTTP listener.
The primary listener (marked with `primary: true`) is used for Pod health checks and Service port generation.

To enable TLS on the HTTP listener:

```yaml
server:
  http:
    - name: https
      primary: true
      value: :8880
      tls: true
      tlsCertFile: /path/to/tls.crt
      tlsKeyFile: /path/to/tls.key
```

To expose an additional listener on a different port:

```yaml
server:
  http:
    - name: http
      primary: true
      value: :8880
    - name: https
      value: :8881
      tls: true
      tlsCertFile: /path/to/tls.crt
      tlsKeyFile: /path/to/tls.key
```

## Resource Naming

By default, this chart uses release-name based resource naming for historical reasons and
backwards compatibility with existing deployments.

> [!WARNING]
> For new deployments, it is recommended to set `useLegacyNaming: false` to use operator-style
> names (e.g. `vmalert-<release>`). This aligns resource names with the
> [VictoriaMetrics operator](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/operator/) convention and
> simplifies future migration to operator-managed resources.

### VictoriaMetrics operator

With `operator.enabled: true` the chart renders a [VMAlert](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/operator/resources/vmalert/) resource
instead of its own vmalert and Alertmanager Deployments/StatefulSets, Services, ServiceAccounts, rules ConfigMap, Alertmanager config Secret, PodDisruptionBudget and VPA,
and the [VictoriaMetrics operator](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/operator/) manages the workload.
The operator must already be installed in the cluster. Operator mode always uses operator-style names, regardless of `useLegacyNaming`.

The VMAlert is named after the release (or `fullnameOverride`), so the operator creates `vmalert-<release>` objects.
Ingress and HTTPRoute are still rendered by the chart and target them. Instead of rendering its own scrape object, the chart lets the operator create the VMServiceScrape when `serviceMonitor.enabled` is enabled, and disables it otherwise.

Settings without an operator equivalent fail rendering, with a message pointing to the replacement:

- `alertmanager.configMap`; use `alertmanager.configSecret` with an `alertmanager.yaml` key instead;
- inline credentials and options without an operator field in `server.datasource`, `server.remoteWrite`, `server.remoteRead` and `server.notifier(s)`; set them in `operator.spec`, referencing Secrets;
- `serviceMonitor` `relabelings`, `metricRelabelings`, `port`, `targetPort`, `path`, `interval`, `scrapeTimeout`, `scheme`, `basicAuth` and `tlsConfig`; set `operator.spec.serviceScrapeSpec` instead;
- `server.networkPolicy` and `alertmanager.networkPolicy` labels, annotations and `extraLabels`, since the operator-managed NetworkPolicy has no custom metadata.

Fields that have no chart value, or need a different value, go to `operator.spec`, which is deep-merged over the generated spec:

```yaml
operator:
  enabled: true
  spec:
    evaluationInterval: 30s
```

Probes keep the operator defaults. Switching a release to `operator.enabled: true` recreates its pods.

